-**(2) α-cell stimulation and unopposed glucagon in T1D.** Amino acids — particularly arginine, alanine, glutamine and leucine — are direct glucagon secretagogues. Intravenous arginine produces 5- to 10-fold increases in plasma glucagon; oral protein produces a more modest 1.5- to 3-fold increase, with magnitude scaling with the amino-acid absorption rate ([Bengtsen & Møller, 2021](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34405569/)). In healthy subjects the simultaneous β-cell amino-acid response (insulin secretion) (a) restrains α-cell glucagon release through paracrine signalling and (b) suppresses hepatic glucose production directly; the net postprandial glucose excursion attributable to protein is therefore small. In T1D the β-cell response is absent, and the α-cell glucagon response to oral protein is largely **preserved** — unlike the glucagon response to hypoglycemia, which is lost within ~5 years of diagnosis ([Bengtsen & Møller, 2021](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34405569/)). The result is unopposed hepatic glucose output (glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis) over 2–5 h. Hormonal regulation and substrate supply can both contribute. The Smart and Paterson experiments below do not quantify which contribution dominates in a given T1D meal.
+**(2) α-cell stimulation and a possible hepatic contribution in T1D.** Amino acids — particularly arginine, alanine, glutamine and leucine — are direct glucagon secretagogues. Intravenous arginine produces 5- to 10-fold increases in plasma glucagon; oral protein produces a more modest 1.5- to 3-fold increase, with magnitude scaling with the amino-acid absorption rate ([Bengtsen & Møller, 2021](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34405569/)). In healthy subjects the simultaneous β-cell amino-acid response (insulin secretion) (a) restrains α-cell glucagon release through paracrine signalling and (b) suppresses hepatic glucose production directly; the net postprandial glucose excursion attributable to protein is therefore small. In T1D the β-cell response is absent, and the α-cell glucagon response to oral protein is largely **preserved** — unlike the glucagon response to hypoglycemia, which is lost within ~5 years of diagnosis ([Bengtsen & Møller, 2021](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34405569/)). Preserved glucagon secretion and amino-acid substrate supply could increase endogenous glucose production, but direct T1D measurements of protein-induced hepatic flux are sparse; neither its magnitude nor a 2–5 h hepatic time course follows from a delayed glucose-concentration rise ([Dao et al., 2025](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39951019/)). The Smart and Paterson experiments below do not partition these candidate mechanisms.
