-Soluble insulin in pharmaceutical formulation exists as a concentration-dependent mixture of zinc-coordinated hexamers (~36 kDa), dimers (~12 kDa), and monomers (~6 kDa). The capillary endothelium of subcutaneous tissue is permeable to monomers and dimers but excludes hexamers; absorption is therefore rate-limited by the hexamer → dimer → monomer dissociation that follows dilution of the depot in interstitial fluid. Oligomer distributions depend on formulation and concentration. The exact concentration cut-offs and oligomer-specific absorption times attributed to Søeborg et al. are not retained because the full paper was not acquired; the general association mechanism is discussed in [Gradel et al. (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30116732/).
+Soluble insulin in pharmaceutical formulation exists as a concentration-dependent mixture of zinc-coordinated hexamers (~36 kDa), dimers (~12 kDa), and monomers (~6 kDa). The capillary endothelium of subcutaneous tissue is permeable to monomers and dimers but excludes hexamers; absorption is therefore rate-limited by the hexamer → dimer → monomer dissociation that follows dilution of the depot in interstitial fluid. Oligomer distributions depend on formulation and concentration. Søeborg et al. (2009) modelled soluble-human-insulin association using equilibrium constants from experiments that differed in formulation, pH, temperature and method. Those concentration relationships are not universal absorption cut-offs for modern analogues or T1D depots. The general association mechanism is also discussed in [Gradel et al. (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30116732/).
