-**Diabetes distress** describes the affective burden specifically arising from living with diabetes -- the unrelenting demands of carbohydrate counting, dosing decisions, fear of hypoglycaemia, fear of long-term complications, perceived clinician unsupportiveness, and family or interpersonal frictions. The 17-item Diabetes Distress Scale (DDS-17) operationalises four subscales: emotional burden, physician-related distress, regimen-related distress, and interpersonal distress ([Polonsky et al., 2005](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15735199/)). In adult T1D cohorts, ~40% report moderate diabetes distress (DDS-17 mean ≥2) and ~20% report high distress (≥3) at any given time, with substantial within-person variation across the disease course ([Fisher et al., 2016 -- T1D longitudinal study](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27118163/)). Distress is statistically and conceptually distinct from clinical depression: in the 3D study, 84% of patients with moderate-to-high diabetes distress did not meet criteria for major depressive disorder, and 67% of those with major depressive disorder also had diabetes distress ([Fisher, Gonzalez & Polonsky, 2014 -- review](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24606397/)).
+**Diabetes distress** describes the affective burden specifically arising from living with diabetes -- the unrelenting demands of carbohydrate counting, dosing decisions, fear of hypoglycaemia, fear of long-term complications, perceived clinician unsupportiveness, and family or interpersonal frictions. The 17-item Diabetes Distress Scale (DDS-17) operationalises four subscales: emotional burden, physician-related distress, regimen-related distress, and interpersonal distress ([Polonsky et al., 2005](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15735199/)). In adult T1D cohorts, ~40% report moderate diabetes distress (DDS-17 mean ≥2) and ~20% report high distress (≥3) at any given time, with substantial within-person variation across the disease course ([Fisher et al., 2016 -- T1D longitudinal study](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27118163/)). Distress is statistically and conceptually distinct from clinical depression. In the separate 3D study of 502 adults with **T2D**, 84.1% of participants with moderate-to-high distress did not meet criteria for major depressive disorder, and 66.7% of those with major depressive disorder also had distress; these overlap proportions must not be presented as T1D estimates ([Fisher, Gonzalez & Polonsky, 2014 -- review](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24606397/)).
