-Systematic within-region rotation is intended to reduce repeated injury and local overuse while limiting arbitrary changes between anatomical regions with different absorption characteristics. Blanco and colleagues' cross-sectional findings associate incorrect rotation with lipohypertrophy; they do not demonstrate prospective incidence. The reported 5% of correct rotators with lipohypertrophy and 98% of people with lipohypertrophy rotating incorrectly have different denominators and are not a risk ratio ([Blanco et al., 2013](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23886784/)). Precise spacing and region schedules belong to clinical-technique guidance and should be checked in the original consensus rather than inferred from that association ([Frid et al., 2016](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27594187/), full-text appraisal pending).
+Systematic within-region rotation is intended to reduce repeated injury and local overuse while limiting arbitrary changes between anatomical regions with different absorption characteristics. Blanco and colleagues' cross-sectional findings associate incorrect rotation with lipohypertrophy; they do not demonstrate prospective incidence. The reported 5% of correct rotators with lipohypertrophy and 98% of people with lipohypertrophy rotating incorrectly have different denominators and are not a risk ratio ([Blanco et al., 2013](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23886784/)). The original FITTER consensus recommends successive injections at least 1 cm apart and avoiding lipohypertrophic areas ([Frid et al., 2016](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27594187/)); this guidance must not be interpreted as a prospective incidence effect from Blanco's cross-sectional data.
